Spring Boot 3 uses the Jakarta namespace: `jakarta.servlet.*`.

Spring Boot 3 is built on Spring Framework 6, which requires Jakarta EE 9 or later. Oracle transferred Java EE to the Eclipse Foundation and the `javax.*` namespace could not move with it, so all the packages were renamed from `javax.*` to `jakarta.*`. Spring Boot 3 sits on Jakarta EE 10, which includes Servlet 6.0.

Typical imports change like this:

```java
// Spring Boot 2.x
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.Filter;

// Spring Boot 3.x
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.Filter;
```

The rename covers more than the servlet API. Other APIs that moved to `jakarta.*` include:

- `javax.persistence.*` → `jakarta.persistence.*` (JPA)
- `javax.validation.*` → `jakarta.validation.*` (Bean Validation)
- `javax.annotation.*` → `jakarta.annotation.*`
- `javax.transaction.*` → `jakarta.transaction.*`

If you're migrating from Spring Boot 2, you'll need to update your imports. Tools like OpenRewrite (the `UpgradeSpringBoot_3_0` recipe) or IntelliJ's migration refactoring can automate most of it. Also check that any third-party libraries you depend on have Jakarta-compatible versions, because libraries still using `javax.*` will not work with the Spring Boot 3 runtime.

